red in yards where  the plane  shattered according to federal  government reports the jets were  supposed to be retired in 2010  but the date was extended to 2030  despite  significant concerns the prime minister  won't say  if the planes will be grounded for good  there is going to be a proper  investigation and we're going to allow  them  to do their work before we make  assumptions about what  might be the outcome of that  investigation the canadian-built tudor  jets have been operating  since the 1960s the snowbirds program  has been used to recruit young people to  the military  like captain richard mcdougall the  34-year-old new brunswick native was  recently interviewed about being the  team's newest member  it's a great little jet to fly i haven't  had too many flights on it yet  but i should be done my conversion  shortly mcdougall has been trained on  other planes and  just became the snowbirds coordinator he  was at the controls of the plane when it  spiraled to  the ground he survived with serious but  non-life-threatening injuries  mitchell too was a young pilot when he  joined the snowbirds in 1999.  it's something i identified as a young  boy as  a prototypically canadian  an identity and now a grief shared by so  many canadians  the planes will likely be on pause for  many months because this investigation  is only just beginning  and could take up to a year donna  robin gill and kamloops bc thank you  a police document unsealed by a judge in  nova scotia  is giving some insight tonight into that  mass shooting in nova scotia  the 40-page redacted document reveals  police believe the gunman  had gathered firearms ammunition  explosives and chemicals  including 800 dollars worth of gasoline  and witnesses who spoke to the police  say the killer was  paranoid about the covet 19 pandemic the  allegations have not been proven in  court  in april 22 people were murdered in a  13-hour rampage across  rural nova scotia the killer was dressed  in an authentic rcmp uniform  and was driving a replica rcmp cruiser  ahead the last tasmanian tiger the newly  released footage of a long  extinct species  more than two million people are being  urged to move inland as supercyclone  ampham heads towards india and  bangladesh it's expected to make  landfall tomorrow  with wind guts of up to 160 kilometers  an hour and heavy rain  pandemic quarantine centers have been  converted into emergency shelters  because the storm is now  a more immediate danger than kovid 19.  a museum in australia has released rare  video of a now  extinct predator this is the last known  footage of a  thylacine known as a tasmanian tiger it  was taken in 1935 his name was benjamin  he lived at a zoo in tasmania  he died a few months after this he  wasn't a tiger at all but a marsupial  they typically had stripes a pouch and a  dog-like head with powerful jaws  they were hunted to extinction  bicycle boom next how covid19 has led  to greener commutes  if we are to harvest british fruit and  vegetables this year  we need an army of people to help  food that's the prince of wales calling  on people to pick for britain  food he says does not happen by magic he  wants students and people laid off  because of covid19 to work with farmers  this summer  without enough help fruits and  vegetables could be left to rot  the campaign is modeled after the second  world war land army  prince charles says he knows it's hard  on glamorous work but it's a vital  contribution to the country  uk farmers rely heavily on migrant  workers as we do here in canada  kovid 19 travel restrictions mean  producers are facing  a shortage of workers  the travel bans and lockdowns to stop  the spread of covid19 have significantly  reduced greenhouse gas emissions  new data published in the journal nature  climate change  shows a 17 drop in daily co2 emissions  in early april compared to the same time  last year  they say this shows just how difficult  it is to lower emissions without 